As of 2019, Gonggong is estimated to have a diameter of {{cvt|1230|km|mi}}, derived from [[Radiometry|radiometric]] measurements, its calculated mass, and assuming a density similar to other similar bodies.<ref name="massdensity"/> This would make Gonggong the fifth-largest trans-Neptunian object, after [[Pluto]], [[Eris (dwarf planet)|Eris]], [[Haumea]] and [[Makemake]]. Gonggong is approximately the size of Pluto's moon [[Charon (moon)|Charon]], although Gonggong's current size estimate has an uncertainty of {{cvt|50|km|mi}}.<ref name="massdensity"/>

Gonggong has been classified as a dwarf planet by several astronomers.<ref name="Tancredi2010"/><ref name="Sheppard-2011"/><ref name="Brown-dplist"/> Brown states that Gonggong "must be a dwarf planet even if predominantly rocky", based on the 2013 radiometric measurement of {{cvt|1290|km|mi}}.<ref name="Brown-dplist"/> [[Scott S. Sheppard|Scott Sheppard]] and colleagues think that it is likely to be a dwarf planet,<ref name="Sheppard-2011"/> based on its minimum possible diameter—{{cvt|580|km|mi}} under the assumption of a completely reflective surface with an albedo of 1{{refn|The resulting minimum diameter of 580&nbsp;km is derived from the equation <math> E=\frac{1329}{\sqrt{p}} 10^{-0.2H}</math>, where <math>H</math> is the absolute magnitude of Gonggong, and <math>p</math> is the albedo of Gonggong, which in this case is assumed to be 1.<ref name="sizemagnitude"/>|name=magnitude|group=lower-alpha}}—and what was at the time the expected lower size limit of around {{cvt|200|km|mi}} for [[hydrostatic equilibrium]] in cold icy-rocky bodies.<ref name="Sheppard-2011"/> However, [[Iapetus (moon)|Iapetus]] is not in equilibrium despite being {{cvt|1470|km|mi}} in diameter, so this remains just a possibility.<ref name="Thomas2010"/>

In 2010, astronomer [[Gonzalo Tancredi]] initially estimated Gonggong to have a very large diameter of {{cvt|1752|km|mi}}, though its dwarf planet status was unclear as there was no lightcurve data or other information to ascertain its size.<ref name="Tancredi2010"/> Gonggong is too distant to be resolved directly; Brown placed a rough estimate of its diameter ranging from {{cvt|1000|-|1500|km|mi}}, based on an albedo of 0.18 which was the best fit in his model.<ref name="Brown-2011"/> A survey led by a team of astronomers using the [[European Space Agency]]'s [[Herschel Space Observatory]] in 2012 determined its diameter to be {{val|1280|210|u=km}} ({{val|795|130|u=mi}}), based on the thermal properties of Gonggong observed in the [[far infrared]] range.<ref name="TNOsCool4"/> This measurement is consistent with Brown's estimate. Later observations in 2013 using combined thermal emission data from ''Herschel'' and the [[Spitzer Space Telescope]] suggested a smaller size of {{val|1142|647|467|u=km}} ({{val|710|402|290|u=mi}}), though this estimate had a larger range of uncertainty.<ref name="TNOsCool9"/>

In 2016, combined observations from the [[Kepler space telescope|''Kepler'']] spacecraft and archival thermal emission data from ''Herschel'' suggested that Gonggong was much larger than previously thought, giving a size estimate of {{val|1535|75|225|u=km}} ({{val|954|46|140|u=mi}}) based on an assumed equator-on view and a lower estimated albedo of 0.089.<ref name="Pal-2016"/><ref name="scida2015"/> This would have made Gonggong the third-largest trans-Neptunian object after Eris and Pluto, larger than Makemake ({{cvt|1430|km|mi}}).<ref name="scida2015"/><ref name="NASA-news-2016"/> These observations of Gonggong were part of the ''Kepler'' spacecraft's [[Kepler space telescope#Second Light (K2)|K2 mission]] which includes studying [[small Solar System bodies]].<ref name="NASA-news-2016"/> Subsequent measurements in 2018 revised the size of Gonggong to {{val|1230|50|u=km}} ({{val|764|31|u=mi}}), based on the mass and density of Gonggong derived from the orbit of its satellite and the discovery that the viewing direction was almost [[axial tilt|pole-on]].<ref name="massdensity"/> With this size estimate, Gonggong is again thought to be the fifth-largest trans-Neptunian object.<ref name="massdensity"/>

=== Mass, density and rotation ===

Based on the orbit of its satellite, the mass of Gonggong has been calculated to be {{cvt|1.75e21|kg|lbs}}, with a density of {{val|1.74|0.16|u=g/cm3}}.<ref name="massdensity"/> Given the mass, the 2016 size estimate of {{cvt|1535|km|mi}} would have implied an unexpectedly low (and likely erroneous) density of {{val|0.92|u=g/cm3}}.<ref name="massdensity"/>

Gonggong is the fifth most massive trans-Neptunian object, after Eris, Pluto, Haumea, and Makemake.<ref name="massdensity"/> It is slightly more massive and denser than Charon, which has a mass of {{cvt|1.586e21|kg|lbs}} and a density of {{val|1.702|u=g/cm3}}.<ref name="massdensity"/><ref name="Stern_2017"/> Due to its large size, mass, and density, Gonggong is expected to be in hydrostatic equilibrium, taking the shape of a [[MacLaurin spheroid]] that is slightly [[oblateness|flattened]] due to its rotation.<ref name="massdensity"/><ref name="Pal-2016"/>

The rotation period of Gonggong was first measured in March 2016, through observations of variations in its brightness with the ''Kepler'' space telescope.<ref name="Pal-2016"/> Gonggong's light curve [[amplitude]] as observed by ''Kepler'' is small, only varying in brightness by about 0.09 [[magnitude (astronomy)|magnitude]]s.<ref name="Pal-2016"/> The small [[light curve]] amplitude of Gonggong indicates that it is being viewed at a pole-on configuration, further evidenced by the observed inclined orbit of its satellite.<ref name="massdensity"/> The ''Kepler'' observations provided ambiguous values of {{val|44.81|0.37}} and {{val|22.4|0.18}} hours for the rotation period.<ref name="Pal-2016"/><ref name="massdensity"/> Based on a best-fit model for its rotation pole orientation, the value of {{val|22.4|0.18}} hours is thought to be the more plausible one.<ref name="massdensity"/> Gonggong rotates slowly compared to other trans-Neptunian objects, which usually have periods between 6 and 12 hours.<ref name="massdensity"/> Due to its slow rotation, it is expected to have a low [[oblateness]] of 0.03 or 0.007, for rotation periods of 22.4 or 44.81 hours, respectively.<ref name="massdensity"/>

== Satellite ==
[[File:Xiangliu orbiting 225088 Gonggong (2009-2010).jpg|thumb|Hubble images of Gonggong and [[Xiangliu (moon)|Xiangliu]], taken in 2009 and 2010 with the [[Wide Field Camera 3]]]]
{{main|Xiangliu (moon)}}
Following the March 2016 discovery that Gonggong was an unusually slow rotator, the possibility was raised that a satellite may have slowed it down via [[tidal force]]s.<ref name="Lakdawalla2016a"/> The indications of a possible satellite orbiting Gonggong led Csaba Kiss and his team to analyze [[precovery|archival]] Hubble observations of Gonggong.<ref name="Kissetal2017"/> Their analysis of Hubble images taken on 18 September 2010 revealed a faint satellite orbiting Gonggong at a distance of at least {{cvt|15000|km|mi}}.<ref name="DPS48"/> The discovery was announced in a [[Division for Planetary Sciences]] meeting on 17 October 2016.<ref name="naming"/> The satellite is approximately {{cvt|100|km|mi}} in diameter and has an orbital period of 25 days.<ref name="Kissetal2017"/> On 5 February 2020 the satellite was officially named [[Xiangliu (moon)|Xiangliu]], after the nine-headed poisonous snake monster that accompanied Gonggong in Chinese mythology. This naming came at the same time that Gonggong itself was officially named.<ref name="MPC-object"/>

== Exploration ==
It was calculated by [[planetary science|planetary scientist]] [[Amanda Zangari]] that a [[flyby (spaceflight)|flyby]] mission to Gonggong would take a minimum of over 20 years with current rocket capabilities.<ref name="Zangari2018"/> A flyby mission could take just under 25 years using a Jupiter [[gravity assist]], based on a launch date of 2030 or 2031. Gonggong would be approximately 95 AU from the Sun when the spacecraft arrives.<ref name="Zangari2018"/>

